latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a Deep Copy of the Input Grid:\n   - Make a copy of the input grid to avoid modifying the original.\n\n2. Identify All Blue Cells:\n   - Iterate through the grid and collect the coordinates of all blue (8) cells.\n\n3. Group Blue Cells into Shapes:\n   a. Start with any blue cell.\n   b. Use a breadth-first search (BFS) or depth-first search (DFS) to find all connected blue cells.\n   c. Include in the shape any blue cells within a two-cell distance, even if not directly connected.\n   d. Remove the cells of the found shape from the set of all blue cells.\n   e. Repeat steps a-d until all blue cells have been assigned to a shape.\n\n4. Create Outlines for Each Shape:\n   For each shape:\n   a. Identify all adjacent cells (including diagonally adjacent) to the shape's blue cells.\n   b. Filter the adjacent cells to keep only those that are:\n      - Within the grid boundaries\n      - Currently black (0)\n      - Not part of the blue shape itself\n   c. These filtered cells form the outline for the shape.\n\n5. Apply the Outlines:\n   - For each cell in each shape's outline, change its value to red (2) in the copied grid.\n\n6. Return the Transformed Grid:\n   - Return the copied grid with the applied outlines.\n\nAdditional Considerations:\n\n- Implement helper functions for common operations like checking if a cell is within grid boundaries, getting adjacent cells, etc.\n- Use efficient data structures like sets for quick membership testing and removal of cells.\n- Ensure that the outlining process doesn't overwrite any non-black cells.\n- Make sure the solution works for grids of different sizes, including edge cases like 1x1 grids or grids where blue shapes touch the boundaries.\n\nThis plan follows the core idea we identified: grouping nearby blue cells into shapes and then outlining each shape with red cells in the immediately adjacent black cells, considering both orthogonal and diagonal adjacency, without overwriting non-black cells or extending beyond the grid boundaries."
